{
  "abstract": "There is a countable group that is hyperlinear and not sofic. This answers in the negative Open question 3.4 of Pestov's guide to hyperlinear and sofic groups (2008): \"Is every hyperlinear group sofic?\" Soficity and hyperlinearity are stated through finite models: maps to the permutations of a finite set, respectively to unitary matrices, that are multiplicative on a given finite set up to epsilon and separate its elements by 1 - epsilon in normalized Hamming distance, respectively by 2 - epsilon in squared normalized Hilbert-Schmidt distance. The development proves that the first notion is equivalent to the criterion of Pestov's Theorem 3.5 and that the second implies the criterion of his Theorem 3.6. The group is the quotient of GL_3(A) x| St by the subgroup generated by a central element of infinite order, where A is a ring that is free of rank 8 over Z[1/2] with a nilpotent ideal of rank 7, and St is the stabilizer of a vector in GL_3(Z[1/2]). Hyperlinearity of such a central quotient is an instance of a theorem of Thom (2010) and is proved here directly. The non-soficity is the new part: the image of EL_3(A) has Kazhdan's property (T), and eight elements normalizing it act on three of its central elements in a way that no sofic approximation can follow. The proof reuses the Lean developments of OpenAI (Kun's theorem on sofic approximations of Kazhdan groups, cluster groups) and of the group-approximation project (the theorem of Ershov and Jaikin-Zapirain). This formalization does not certify finite generation or finite presentation of the group, the equivalence of the local criteria with the ultraproduct definitions, or anything about other groups.",
